<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khalkha Mongols</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Khalkha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khalkha <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> are the largest subgroup of the Mongols and form the majority of the population of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Organised by the descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayan-khan">Dayan Khan</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geresenje">Geresenje</a>, they were divided from the 16th century into the Tüsheet, Zasagt and Setsen khanates. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> invasions they submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolon-nor">Dolon Nor</a> in 1691. Their dialect is the basis of modern standard Mongolian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols.jpg" alt="Khalkha Mongols" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Esiymbro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongol_Subgroups_Khalkha_1911.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
